  "notes": "A remix-first progressive house producer whose signature the research calls \"harmonic rather than rhythmic\" — the \"Axwell Mix\" is documented as a recognisable treatment applied across genres. So the differentiating authorship here is on the chord and melody side: minor vamps with a lifted major-mode lead melody, the widest seventh weight of the three trio members, and 8- to 16-bar loops. The working method is the other encoded fact: \"as a remixer he retains the original vocal and rebuilds the rhythm section beneath it\", and \"his remixes typically extend the build and delay the drop relative to the original\" — which is a 16-bar prechorus and a longer intro than his partners'. The Supermode alias is named as \"the disco/vocal-sample-forward side of the same approach\", which is why the perc bed keeps a shaker and the hats open wider than the big-room field. Bass is \"sidechained saw or pluck; sub removed under the breakdown\". ⚠ The research marks the harmony claim `[LOW-CONF]` and says the parameters come from the progressive-house convention; both marks are repeated here, and every velocity, density and register above is INFERRED.",
